WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.060
As we learn more about Avengers: Endgame,
Marvel's skillful foreshadowing becomes clearer.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:08.900
The MCU has been preparing us for this grand
climax for years already — we just didn't

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:09.400
know it.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.200
From early title drops to ominous plot clues,
we're cataloging the ways the MCU has been

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.780
building to its Endgame.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.780
The phrase "endgame" has appeared in the MCU
long before Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.770
The first time was in a line spoken by Tony
Stark during Avengers: Age of Ultron back

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:24.840
in 2015.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.730
The line comes up when several of the Avengers
criticize Stark for creating Ultron.

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.550
Thor is so enraged by Tony's actions that
he even picks him up by the throat before

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.310
Captain America talks him down.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.430
In response, Tony launches into a tirade about
how unprepared they are for a real threat

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:39.460
from an alien enemy:

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:40.580
"We're the Avengers.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.520
We can bust arms dealers all the livelong
day, but… that, up there?

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.560
That's the endgame."

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.580
As it turns out, Stark is more right than
anyone in that moment realizes.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.030
When Stark uses the phrase "endgame" while
gesturing towards space, he's foreshadowing

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.570
the showdown with Thanos.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.399
The more recent reference to "Endgame" came
during Avengers: Infinity War, when Doctor

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:05.489
Strange uses the Time Stone to view possible
outcomes of their battle with Thanos.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.470
Despite seeing millions of different potential
futures, Strange is only able to find one

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:10.790
where the Avengers win.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.640
Once the Sorcerer Supreme knows the path to
the one future scenario in which Thanos is

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.750
defeated, his every move is a calculated decision
to bring about that future.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.670
This means that when Strange willingly exchanges
the Time Stone to Thanos for Stark's life

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.840
in Avengers: Infinity War, he has a reason
for giving it up.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.930
When a devastated Tony asks why he gave in
to Thanos, Strange has a simple answer:

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:31.920
"We're in the endgame now.”

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.940
In other words, Avengers: Endgame has been
a long time coming.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.030
During the press conference scene midway through
the first Iron Man film back in 2008, Tony

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.300
Stark mentions how he never got to say goodbye
to his father.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.080
"I never got to say goodbye to dad.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.060
I never got to say goodbye to my father."

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.720
Losing his parents so suddenly at such a young
age left a psychological scar that Tony never

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:55.660
truly healed from.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.170
This trauma came up directly during Tony's
early scene in Captain America: Civil War.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:04.150
Tony creates an entire virtual simulation
with which he can relive the last day he saw

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:05.180
his parents alive.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.800
In the simulation, Tony's mother urges him
to say goodbye to his father.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.460
"You know what's about to happen.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:12.780
Say something.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.680
If you don't you'll regret it."

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.840
Tony clearly never moved on from how he treated
his father the last time he saw him, and he's

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.060
obviously always wanted a chance to say goodbye.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.880
During his time travel escapades in Endgame,
Tony finally gets his wish.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.920
In Avengers: Age of Ultron, several of the
Avengers attempt to lift Thor's hammer, Mjolnir.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:35.439
Even though nearly every hero can't get it
to move, the hammer actually shifts when Steve

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.620
Rogers tries to lift it.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.700
The movement is so subtle that none of the
other Avengers seem to notice it, but Thor

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:41.240
sure does.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.980
That small detail is why Thor is the only
one in the film, and in the audience, that

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.880
isn't surprised when Cap finally wields the
hammer against Thanos in Avengers: Endgame.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.280
Thor knew that Captain America was worthy
to lift Mjolnir all along.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.340
When Steve and Tony got in each other's face
back when the scepter was influencing the

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:01.900
Avengers in the first film, they had a bitter
exchange that foreshadowed both of their futures.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.420
"The only thing you really fight for is yourself.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.420
You're not the guy to make the sacrifice play."

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:08.760
"A hero?

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:09.720
Like you?

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.939
You're a laboratory experiment, Rogers.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.180
Everything special about you came from a bottle."

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.720
Both these assumptions are torn apart during
the events of Endgame.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.840
When they first meet, Cap doesn't believe
that Tony has what it takes to lay his life

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:27.269
on the line for others, but in Avengers: Endgame,
Tony sacrifices himself to save half of the

00:03:27.269 --> 00:03:28.870
life in the entire universe.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.010
Meanwhile, when Steve is able to lift Mjolnir
during Avengers: Endgame, it means the hammer

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:35.599
has deemed him worthy of possessing Thor's
power.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.260
The fact that Captain America is considered
worthy enough to wield the power of a god

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.740
proves that Steve Rogers is a hero without
his Super Soldier Serum.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:48.000
Toward the start of Avengers: Infinity War,
Tony mentions a dream he had to Pepper as

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.620
they're walking together in Central Park:

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.940
"I dreamt we had a kid.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.420
It was so real.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.900
We named him after your eccentric uncle.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.180
Uh, what was his name?"

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:57.700
"Morgan."

00:03:58.380 --> 00:03:58.920
"Right."

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.560
Although Tony gets the gender wrong in his
dream, this scene foreshadows Tony becoming

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.340
a father when we see him again in Avengers:
Endgame.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.190
Morgan Stark is actually a character from
the Iron Man comics but is neither Pepper's

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:11.180
uncle nor her daughter with Tony.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:15.620
Instead, Morgan Stark is actually Tony Stark's
cousin who becomes involved in various supervillain

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:16.120
plots.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.640
Although naming Tony Stark's daughter after
his nefarious cousin might seem like a strange

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.780
Easter egg, it shows how much the filmmakers
still respect their character's humble comic

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.080
book beginnings.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.380
Throughout the Iron Man films, Pepper is always
making sure Tony is getting enough sleep.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.130
In Iron Man 2, she tells him to go to bed
after he gets out of control at his birthday

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:34.640
party.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.460
"It's time to go to bed.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.240
It's time."

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.540
In Iron Man 3, Tony Stark's insomnia isn't
coming from an interest in his work or too

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.220
much partying; it's because he's terrified
of what dangers are lurking in the galaxy.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.800
"But honey, I can't sleep.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:51.100
You go to bed, I come down here.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:51.900
I do what I know.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:53.820
I tinker."

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.080
Even the first Iron Man had a brief line in
the original script where Pepper is surprised

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:01.340
to see that Tony has been focused on his armor
instead of sleeping.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:05.970
With all those moments of Tony's inability
to actually rest, Pepper's final words to

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.300
Tony, when she tells him he can rest, carry
even more meaning.

